# 6 Evaluation

<!-- Generated by tools/hls-est-validate/thesis/make_thesis_tables.py from templates/06-evaluation.md.in on {{generated}}.
     Do not edit: every number below is a placeholder filled from the result files named in hls-doc/thesis/README.md.
     Newest run: {{newest_run}} (hls-est {{newest_hlsest_git}}, cgeist md5 {{newest_cgeist_md5}}). -->

This chapter measures how closely hls-est reproduces the BRAM decisions of Vitis HLS 2025.1. It covers six questions: how accurate the newest version is (6.2); how the accuracy developed over the rounds of the method (6.3); what the errors look like (6.4); which modelled rules contribute how much (6.5); how fast hls-est is compared with a Vitis run (6.6); and which designs are still out of band, and why (6.7). Section 6.8 lists what the evaluation does not cover.

## 6.1 Set-up

**Ground truth.** Every Vitis number is the `csynth` BRAM_18K total of a Vitis HLS 2025.1 run. All runs use the part `xc7z020clg400-1`, a 10 ns clock, `flow_target=vivado` and `syn.array_partition.throughput_driven=off`, and are stored in `results/vitis.json`. The parser reads the `* Memory:` utilisation table and `csynth.xml`, never the storage report (see the harness guide).

**Corpus.** The corpus is the HLSFactory_v2 catalogue: {{all_designs}} designs in {{datasets_n}} datasets. Two designs are counted as copies only when they have the same cgeist MLIR byte for byte, the same top function and the same Vitis total. By that test, {{duplicate_copies}} of the compared designs are copies of another compared design: the `WIP` dataset repeats `stream_hls` designs, and `polybench__reproducible` repeats some of its own. The tables therefore also report a *distinct sources* view.

**Pipeline.** Every design goes through cgeist with the real Vitis headers (`--shim real`), then through the default hls-est pipeline, under a limit of 120 s per design. Each run is one result file of `run_hlsest.py`.

**Metrics.** A design is *compared* when both tools return a BRAM total and the hls-est record was produced from the source Vitis synthesised. It is *exact* when the two totals are equal. It is *in band* when it is exact or over-counts by at most {{band_over_pct}} % of the Vitis value; under-counts are always out of band (definition of done, item 5). The other metrics are:
- Σ|err|: the sum of |hls-est − Vitis| over compared designs.
- *Weighted error*: Σ|err| divided by the summed Vitis BRAM.
- *Relative error*: (hls-est − Vitis) / Vitis, defined only for designs where Vitis reports at least one BRAM.

**Re-scoring.** Every run in this chapter is re-scored against the current `results/vitis.json` by `thesis_data.score`, which applies the same rules as `compare.py`. For the newest run, the generator checks that the re-scoring reproduces the run's own `compare.py` summary; the check reports: {{rescore_check}}. Older runs can differ from the numbers recorded at the time by a design or two (Table 6.3, column "as recorded"). The cause is ground-truth entries merged or re-keyed since those runs.

## 6.2 Headline accuracy

{{table:headline}}

*Table 6.1. Headline accuracy (generated: `tables/ch06-headline.md`, `.tex`).*

Run {{newest_label}} covers {{all_designs}} designs:
- cgeist compiles {{all_cgeist_ok}} of them, and hls-est returns an estimate for {{all_hlsest_ok}}. cgeist fails on {{all_cgeist_fail}} designs; `done-exclusions.md` matches them to the Vitis rejections of the same sources. Of the compiled designs, {{all_hlsest_fail}} end without an estimate: in {{all_top_not_found}} hls-est finds no function with the top's name, and {{all_hlsest_timeout}} reach the 120 s limit.
- Vitis has no result for {{all_vitis_missing}} of the designs hls-est estimates: Vitis rejected them, failed or timed out.
- {{all_compared}} designs are compared. Of these, {{all_exact}} ({{all_exact_pct}} %) are exact and {{all_in_band}} ({{all_in_band_pct}} %) are in band.
- Of the {{all_out_of_band}} designs out of band, {{all_under}} are under-counts and {{all_over_gt15}} over-count by more than {{band_over_pct}} %.
- Over all compared designs, Σ|err| = {{all_abs_err}} BRAM_18K against {{all_vitis_bram_total}} BRAM_18K that Vitis allocates, a weighted error of {{all_weighted_err_pct}} %. The mean |relative error| is {{all_mean_abs_rel_pct}} %. Among the designs that are not exact, the median |relative error| is {{all_median_abs_rel_nonexact_pct}} %.

Three things qualify these numbers.
1. **Zero-BRAM designs.** For {{all_compared_vitis_zero}} compared designs, Vitis reports zero BRAM, and hls-est matches {{all_compared_vitis_zero_exact}} of them. These designs are not trivial for the model: a zero means that every array is a register, a LUTRAM (N5) or was removed (N1, N1a, N66). Still, they lift the exact rate. Over the {{nonzero_compared}} designs where Vitis reports at least one BRAM, {{nonzero_exact}} are exact and {{nonzero_in_band}} are in band.
2. **Copies.** Counting each distinct source once, {{distinct_exact}} of {{distinct_compared}} designs are exact and {{distinct_in_band}} are in band. The weighted error is {{distinct_weighted_err_pct}} %.
3. **Weighted error.** The weighted error is low because the largest designs (stream_hls and its WIP copies, hlsyn-vitis) dominate the summed Vitis BRAM, and those designs are mostly exact. The per-dataset table shows where the remaining error lies.

The harness subset is the original development set: polybench, polybench_pragmas, forgebench, machsuite, spector_hls and vitis_examples. On it, {{subset_exact}} of {{subset_compared}} designs are exact and {{subset_in_band}} are in band, with Σ|err| = {{subset_abs_err}}.

{{table:per_dataset}}

*Table 6.2. Accuracy per dataset (`tables/ch06-per_dataset.md`).*

The per-dataset view separates two kinds of source:
- **Benchmark suites written for HLS tools.** polybench, polybench_pragmas, spector_hls, soda, hp_fft_hls and vitis_examples are exact on every compared design. machsuite has {{ds_machsuite_exact}} of {{ds_machsuite_compared}} exact, and forgebench {{ds_forgebench_exact}} of {{ds_forgebench_compared}}.
- **Student course projects.** The three `class_ece8893_*` datasets hold the hardest cases. In `class_ece8893_spring_2025`, {{ds_class_ece8893_spring_2025_exact}} of {{ds_class_ece8893_spring_2025_compared}} designs are exact, {{ds_class_ece8893_spring_2025_in_band}} are in band, and Σ|err| = {{ds_class_ece8893_spring_2025_abs_err}}. These designs combine dataflow regions, hls::stream calls inside pipelined loops, struct and vector types and hand-placed pragmas, which is where most of the open issues lie (Section 6.7).

## 6.3 Accuracy over the rounds

![Accuracy history](figures/ch06-history.png)

*Figure 6.1. Compared, exact and in-band designs, and Σ|err| (log scale), after each round (`figures/ch06-history.pdf`). The dotted line marks the switch from the exactclass shim to the real Vitis headers.*

{{table:history}}

*Table 6.3. Accuracy after each round (`tables/ch06-history.md`).*

The history starts at the first full HLSFactory_v2 run ({{history_first}}): {{hist0_exact}} of {{hist0_compared}} designs exact, Σ|err| = {{hist0_abs_err}}. The newest run ({{history_last}}) has {{histN_exact}} of {{histN_compared}} exact and Σ|err| = {{histN_abs_err}}. The curve has three phases:

1. **Structural channel rules (FIFO, PIPO).** The steep rise comes from hls::stream FIFOs (N31) and dataflow ping-pong buffers (N33). Before these rules, the model priced both kinds of channel as ordinary RAMs, or not at all. Each rule changed the estimate of many dataflow designs at once (Table 6.4).
2. **Memory-architecture rules.** Exactness then rises more slowly through RAM width narrowing (N35), process merges (N36), the inputs of the RAM_1WNR copy count (N38) and struct handling (N37). The last of these is the large Σ|err| drop at "N37".
3. **The switch to the real Vitis headers (W11).** The number of compiled designs rises from {{histR0_cgeist_ok}} to {{histR1_cgeist_ok}}, and the number compared from {{histR0_compared}} to {{histR1_compared}}. Σ|err| rises from {{histR0_abs_err}} to {{histR1_abs_err}}. Per the W11-adopt CHANGELOG entry, the rise comes from the designs compared only under the real headers, and no exact design was lost. Rounds W9r6–W9r10 then bring Σ|err| down again, each with a few proven rules.

The rise of Σ|err| at W9r8 has a known cause, recorded in the W9r8 CHANGELOG entry. A cgeist fix to cross-unit inlining (N68) exposed a separate banking problem on two Yolo-v3-tiny designs, and the window-read rule N74 of W9r9 resolved it.

## 6.4 Error distribution

![Error distribution](figures/ch06-error_distribution.png)

*Figure 6.2. Left: the signed relative error of the designs that are not exact. Right: the cumulative distribution of |relative error| over all compared designs with a non-zero Vitis total. The dashed line is the {{band_over_pct}} % band (`figures/ch06-error_distribution.pdf`).*

![hls-est vs Vitis](figures/ch06-scatter.png)

*Figure 6.3. hls-est against Vitis per compared design, on log-log axes (BRAM + 1, so that zero totals can be drawn). The solid line is equality and the dashed line the {{band_over_pct}} % band (`figures/ch06-scatter.pdf`).*

The distribution is sharply peaked at zero. Of the designs that are not exact, most are within a few percent, and the over-counts cluster just above zero, inside the band. The out-of-band points fall into two groups:
- **Under-counts**, which make up most of them ({{all_under}} of {{all_out_of_band}}). The largest of them come from loops that Vitis pipelines but hls-est leaves sequential or schedules at a higher II, so a memory gets fewer copies (Section 6.7).
- **A few large relative over-counts on small designs.** For example, whetstone has 0 BRAM in Vitis, so any non-zero estimate is out of band.

## 6.5 What each rule contributes

Table 6.4 measures each modelled rule by a pair of full-catalogue runs that differ only in that rule. Most rules since W9r7 have a configuration key, so the "off" run sets that key to its earlier value on the same MLIR. These are the "one key off" and "config key chain" rows; the chain t0…t10 of W9r7 switches the rules on one at a time. Earlier rules are measured between consecutive runs of the history ("consecutive" rows).

Only designs compared in both runs count. "changed" is the number of designs whose estimate moved; "band in / out" counts designs that entered or left the band. The "evidence" column gives the status of each N-note in VITIS_NOTES. A rule is modelled only after a minimal-kernel proof, so every N-row is "confirmed". Rows labelled O… implement an open issue without a separate Vitis note.

{{table:rule_contributions}}

*Table 6.4. Per-rule contribution, ranked by the change of Σ|err| (`tables/ch06-rule_contributions.md`; the newly-exact design lists are in `data/ch06-numbers.json`).*

![Rule contributions](figures/ch06-rule_contributions.png)

*Figure 6.4. Designs newly exact and Σ|err| reduction per rule (`figures/ch06-rule_contributions.pdf`).*

The largest contributors are {{rule1_name}} ({{rule1_notes}}: Σ|err| {{rule1_derr}}, {{rule1_newly}} designs newly exact), {{rule2_name}} ({{rule2_notes}}: {{rule2_derr}}) and {{rule3_name}} ({{rule3_notes}}: {{rule3_derr}}). Most later rules are narrow. They fix one memory pattern that appears in a handful of designs. That is expected once the broad mechanisms are in place, and it is why progress per round slowed.

Most rules lose no design that was exact. In {{rules_lost_any}} rows a design stops being exact, {{rules_lost_total}} designs in all: {{rules_lost_list}}. Each case has a per-array explanation, as working-loop rule 4 requires:
- The PIPO case is recorded in the W9-PIPO CHANGELOG entry. The design had been exact only by cancellation: its channel array now equals Vitis, and a second array is still priced wider than the width Vitis narrows it to (O18).
- arati lab2 is recorded in the W9r7 report: an array that now equals Vitis had cancelled an unmatched one (O54 (b)).
- hp_fft `original_C_style` is exact again one step later in the same chain, once its math core is modelled (N60).

{{rules_zero_effect}} rules change no catalogue estimate: {{rules_zero_list}}. Both are confirmed on their kernels and kept for correctness. The catalogue has no design whose BRAM total depends on them alone; N65's catalogue design still has the wrong II (O87).

Two rows make Σ|err| worse while being per-array correct:
- **N48** (each dataflow call site is its own instance). It is right per array: the conv processes' arrays are now counted once per call site, as in Vitis. But the new instances also multiply per-instance arrays that Vitis keeps out of BRAM (O67), an excess that the lower instance count used to hide.
- **The real-headers frontend.** It replaces a hand-written shim with the Vitis headers. That trades a few totals for correct widths and for coverage, which is the decision recorded as MEMOS #66.

{{table:pass_ablation}}

*Table 6.5. Pass ablation of the W9r5 pipeline, under the exactclass shim (`tables/ch06-pass_ablation.md`).*

The ablation removes one pass at a time from the default pipeline. Without the pass whose removal costs most, {{ablation_top_pass}}, {{ablation_top_lost}} designs stop being exact. Banking, the automatic pipeline / unroll / flatten decision (N3) and the loop scheduler carry the model. Without the decision Σ|err| is {{ablation_apu_ratio}}× that of the full pipeline, and without the scheduler {{ablation_sched_ratio}}×, because RAM_1WNR replication (N4) depends on the schedule's II. {{ablation_zero}} passes have no effect on their own. They are clean-up passes (canonicalisation, constant propagation, loop normalisation) that change no estimate on their own.

## 6.6 Run time

{{table:runtime}}

*Table 6.6. Run time per design (`tables/ch06-runtime.md`).*

![Run time](figures/ch06-runtime.png)

*Figure 6.5. Left: the cumulative distributions of the hls-est and Vitis times per design. Right: the cumulative distribution of the speed-up (`figures/ch06-runtime.pdf`).*

{{time_n}} designs have both times. hls-est takes a median of {{time_hlsest_median_s}} s per design, and {{time_hlsest_under_1s}} designs finish in under 1 s. A Vitis run takes a median of {{time_vitis_median_s}} s. The median speed-up is {{time_speedup_median}}×; the 10th percentile is {{time_speedup_p10}}×. Over the whole corpus, Vitis needs {{time_vitis_total_h}} h, and hls-est is {{time_speedup_total}}× faster in total. hls-est is slower than Vitis on {{time_slower_than_vitis}} designs. {{time_hlsest_over_60s}} designs take more than 60 s, and the slowest stop at the 120 s limit. All of them are large designs whose dependence analysis or scheduling is still slow (O103, O105).

The comparison is not like for like:
- The hls-est time covers only the estimation. It does not include the cgeist front end, which `run_hlsest.py` does not time separately.
- The Vitis time is the wall time of the whole Vitis run on the Windows host. That includes start-up and C synthesis, but not RTL export.
- The harness runs eight hls-est jobs at once in WSL, while Vitis runs were scheduled differently.

The speed-up shows orders of magnitude. It is not a benchmark.

## 6.7 Designs still out of band

{{table:out_of_band}}

*Table 6.7. The {{all_out_of_band}} out-of-band designs of run {{newest_label}}, grouped by the cause class with the largest net error in the round-10 ranking (`tables/ch06-out_of_band.md`; every design in `tables/ch06-out_of_band_designs.md`).*

The classes come from the W9r10-prove ranking (OPEN_ISSUES O113). That ranking split each out-of-band design's error per array and assigned each part to a cause. "changed since ranking" counts designs whose total has moved since the ranked run. For those, the class records the dominant cause at ranking time.

The largest class is {{oob1_class}}: {{oob1_designs}} designs, Σ|d| {{oob1_sumd}}. These are loops that contain hls::stream reads or writes. hls-est leaves such loops sequential, while Vitis pipelines them (O54 (c)). The stream timing is proven (N82) and implemented, but it stays opt-in. Switched on, it loses many designs that are exact today, because it waits for loop-carried memory recurrences (O1, O114), so it fails working-loop rule 4. The next classes are:
- {{oob2_class}}: the greedy modulo scheduler's II on large bodies (O56).
- {{oob3_class}}: an unroll factor on a variable-trip pipelined loop.

Smaller classes are either proven rules that are not implemented yet, such as flattening across a stream access (N55), or rules whose Vitis trigger is itself unconfirmed: the do-while flatten refusal (N64) is unconfirmed as to which source shape causes it.

Out-of-band designs total Σ|d| = {{oob_sumd}}. {{oob_under}} of them are under-counts. The largest under-count classes are schedule-driven: an II that is too high, or a loop that is not pipelined, gives fewer RAM_1WNR copies (N4, N38). The over-counts are spread over several classes: RAM_1WNR copy counts (K1e), arrays Vitis keeps in registers or removes (K13, O60) and an m_axi adapter whose INTERFACE pragma Vitis drops (K4c).

## 6.8 Threats to validity and what is not covered

- **BRAM only.** hls-est models BRAM_18K, the resource with the most discrete and most explainable allocation. DSP, FF and LUT (Phase 6) are not evaluated here.
- **One target, one tool version, one mode.** The ground truth is Vitis 2025.1 on a single Zynq-7000 part (`xc7z020`) at 10 ns with `throughput_driven=off`. The `td=auto` behaviour differs (N2), for example in automatic array splitting, and is only partly modelled. The formula is the 7-series RAMB18 aspect-mode formula (N6, N8). For other families it is supplied as data by the configuration (`hls-doc/new-architecture.md`), but no other family has been measured against its tool. Many designs exceed the device: for {{vitis_over_device}} compared designs, Vitis reports more BRAM than the part has. The evaluation compares allocations, not implementable designs.
- **An academic corpus.** HLSFactory_v2 is mostly benchmark kernels and student projects. Some of it is duplicated (Section 6.1), and {{all_compared_vitis_zero}} compared designs have no BRAM at all. Industrial designs, with deep call hierarchies, large IP libraries and many interface variants, are not represented.
- **Development on the evaluation set.** The rules were found while working on the same catalogue they are scored on; there is no held-out test set. Three things mitigate this:
  - No rule may branch on design names, array names or specific sizes.
  - Every rule is first proven on minimal kernels that are not catalogue designs, and the kernel counts are listed in each N-note.
  - Rules that fail the catalogue gate stay opt-in (for example N82).

  Even so, the exact-match rate partly measures how well the corpus has been studied. A fresh corpus would measure generalisation.
- **Totals, not every array.** Exactness is judged on design totals. In a design that is exact, a per-array mismatch can still cancel against another. The per-array comparison of `compare.py` and the CHANGELOG entries document several such cases (for example arati lab2 in W9r7). They are not counted here.
- **Ground-truth limits.** Some Vitis runs failed, timed out or were rejected (`done-exclusions.md`). Their designs are not compared, and the 120 s hls-est limit excludes a few large designs.
